  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Solve the inequality $2{(0.7)}^{x+9}\\ge 4.$",
  "worked_solution": "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(0.7)}^{x+9} & \\ge 4 \\\\ {(0.7)}^{x+9} & \\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061[(0.7)^{x+9}] & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}\u20612 \\\\ (x+9)\\operatorname{ln}\u20610.7 & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}\u20612.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}\u20610.7$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+9 & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}{\\operatorname{ln}\u20610.7} \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}{\\operatorname{ln}\u20610.7}-9\\end{aligned}\n$$",
